WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.880
Welcome to Foresight. My guest is John Curiacco. He's the author of three books of which you

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:25.840
can see behind us. John comes to us at a very difficult time in our country. John, if anything

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.640
exemplifies what we seem to be lacking, not only in our politicians, but in our media,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:43.920
in our schools sometimes, in our just general daily process of life. And that is honesty, patriotism

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.920
and dealing with very, very difficult subjects.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.680
I asked John to be here today to enlighten us a little bit about the three books that he's

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:04.160
already written. But I mostly asked him to be able to speak of who he is and what constitutes a person

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:12.640
who would put himself in his life and his loved ones at jeopardy. And that is not what we normally

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:19.840
think of people doing. But there are those out there, there are those people, both men and women

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.600
who do that, who jeopardize everything for truth and honesty. John, welcome to the show.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.920
Thanks very much for having me. It is indeed a pleasure.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.520
So as we were saying earlier, what I'd like you to do is just to pick your first book,

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.000
which I believe is The Red Lucked in Spy, and sort of give us a praisey of what the story is

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.400
about. Sure. And we'll just go through each one like that, and then we'll get to

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.800
more fun things afterwards. Sounds good. The first book is The Red Lucked in Spy,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.960
My Secret Life in the CIA's War on Terror. I was in the CIA from January of 1990

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:06.160
until mid-2004. The first half of my career was in analysis, Middle Eastern analysis focused

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.200
almost solely on Iraq before most Americans had ever heard of Iraq. And the second half

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.280
of my career was in counter-terrorism operations at first, and it's kind of quaint now,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.800
but first targeting European communist terrorist groups. And then of course,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.280
like everybody else in the intelligence community, moving on to al-Qaeda and other

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:34.800
Sunni extremist groups. I left the CIA in 2004 and went into the private sector.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:45.120
After I left the CIA, I waited for somebody, anybody, to reveal the existence of the torture

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:53.520
program, which I believed to be illegal, immoral, unethical, certainly. And nobody said anything.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.920
And you didn't just come to that conclusion that it was more illegal. I mean, there were

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:05.280
already written pages of why that was not a legal thing to do.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.360
Oh, very clearly written pages.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.480
From, what is it, Nuremberg?

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.200
That's right. From Nuremberg. In 1946, Congress passed a law called the Federal

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:20.160
Torture Act. And it banned specifically the kinds of techniques that we were using

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:29.840
against al-Qaeda prisoners. In January of 1946, we executed Japanese soldiers who had

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:38.400
waterboarded American prisoners of war. In January of 1968, the Washington Post

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.960
ran a front page photograph of an American soldier waterboarding a Vietnamese,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.000
North Vietnamese prisoner. The day that that photo was published, the Secretary of Defense,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.720
Robert McNamara, ordered an investigation. That soldier was arrested. He was charged with torture,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:02.160
convicted, and sentenced to 20 years at Leavenworth. Well, the law never changed. We changed.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:12.560
So why was waterboarding an executable offense in 1946 punishable by 20 years at hard labor

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:19.840
in 1968, and then suddenly, as if by magic, perfectly legal in 2002? I knew it wasn't legal.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.840
In Robert McNamara, the one who was responsible for the Vietnam War, who would later write a book.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.640
That's right. Begging forgiveness. Begging forgiveness for that.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:35.680
Yes, you're exactly right. A notorious figure in modern American history.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:43.040
But even he knew that this was a bridge too far. It was just too illegal, too obviously,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.840
and clearly illegal. Well, the Bush administration decided that it wasn't illegal,

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.760
and that we were going to use it, and we were going to use it with frequency, which we did.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.560
It was finally again made illegal with passage of the McCain-Feinstein

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:08.720
amendment in the summer of 2015. And the issue, aside from being a humanitarian fault,

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.280
was, I think McCain's attitude was because he had gone through the torture himself,

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.920
that what we would be doing was we would say, well, this is okay. So if we thought it was

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:27.200
okay, then other people would do the same. Precisely. We like to present ourselves to

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:34.400
other countries as a shining beacon of human rights and civil rights and civil liberties,

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.600
and we're not. So we either- That's a hard truth.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.200
Will be, yeah. That's a very hard truth. We either will be or we won't be.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.280
One of the complaints I had when I was at the CIA

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.080
surrounded the State Department's annual Human Rights Report. Congress mandated in 1978

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.280
that every American embassy in the world write a Human Rights Report every year

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.520
on the country that is hosting us, right? So in every country where we have diplomatic

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.560
relations around the world, we write a Human Rights Report, and that report goes to Congress

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:14.320
annually. So imagine you're the State Department Human Rights Officer, which I was for two years

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:22.480
in Bahrain, 1995 and 96, and you go into the Minister of Interior's Office and you tell him

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.240
your Excellency, you cannot just pick up a 15-year-old boy and torture him to death,

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.000
and then call his parents the next day to come and pick up the body. That's an extra

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.920
judicial killing. It's a violation of the law and you can't do it, and I have to put it

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:41.200
in the Human Rights Report. And then 15 minutes later, a CIA officer comes in to meet with

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.240
a minister and he says, don't pay any attention to what that State Department guy said. We want

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:50.480
you to open a secret prison for us so we can bring prisoners here and torture them,

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:57.040
and nobody will know about it. It completely undercuts our international moral authority.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.920
So when we go to other countries and we say, you need to respect human rights,

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.480
they laugh at us because they know that we don't respect human rights.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.480
Yeah, respect begets respect. And it damages the country. Right, right.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:19.600
So either we're going to observe and respect international law or we're not, but we can't do

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:25.040
both. Being respecting the international laws of which we are responsible for having written.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:31.520
I said earlier today in a talk at Hamilton Hall that we were the drafters of the United

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.240
Nations Convention Against Torture. And then we say, well, we don't like this UN Convention

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.840
Against Torture because it won't let us do what we want to do. We wrote the law.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.520
Right. It's kind of like the treaties back in the 17...

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.240
That's right. The Indian treaties. That's right.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:53.200
So what I found fascinating within the realms of the first book was what you had to go

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.160
through in order to get into the CIA. One of the things that I thought was fascinating was

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:04.800
at that time, very few other Asians spoke Arabic. They couldn't even...

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.120
And still very few people speak Arabic.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.080
So you took it upon yourself to learn the language.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:10.480
Yes.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:15.280
And so you're trialing. You speak Greek, you speak English, and you speak Arabic.

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.000
Yes.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:20.160
So when people saw you coming down said, yeah, we need him.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.480
Well, when I made the decision to switch over from analysis to operations,

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.760
I saw a job advertised in Athens. It was a counter-terrorism officer's job.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:36.560
And it said either Greek or English, I'm sorry, either Greek or Arabic preferred,

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:40.480
not mandatory, either Greek or Arabic preferred.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.480
So I found out who was responsible for hiring and I made an appointment.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:48.960
I went down to see him and I said, listen, I'm an analyst and I don't know the first

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.840
thing about operations. But I speak Greek and Arabic fluently, both of them.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.880
He said, get out of here. And I said, no, really.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:57.920
No, you don't really, right?

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:05.680
Well, as it turned out, I was one of only two people in the entire CIA who had those two languages.

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:11.760
And the other one was a language teacher. So I said, I know I'm not qualified for this job,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:17.840
but I'd like to throw my hat in. He said, listen, it's way easier and way cheaper for me

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.320
to hire a linguist and teach him operations than it is for me to take an operations officer

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:25.120
and teach him how to speak Greek and Arabic. And so I got the job.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:33.520
So I wonder that persists today where there are still so few people who understand the language.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:37.840
Well, they go through these fits and starts where we need to go out and hire as many Arabic

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.760
speakers as we can. But then many Arabic speakers are either first-generation Americans

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.800
or they are refugees who became Americans. And so they have trouble with the polygraph

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.160
or with the background exam and they end up not getting hired. So what has happened at the CIA

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:04.720
is now a plurality of the Arabic speakers are Arab Christians.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:08.160
mostly Lebanese, Coptic Egyptians, who have the language don't have the counterintelligence

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:11.880
problems that might be associated with someone from those countries.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:17.120
They get hired, but then they have this innate bias against Muslims in their own country.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:23.240
And so it's kind of hard to use them operationally because they're hard to control.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:24.480
Exactly.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:35.040
So the evening upon the capture, this is the part where I was, you know, I couldn't put the

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.040
book down.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:38.560
I mean, okay, all right.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:40.560
So give us a little bit about the build up.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.840
I mean, I know you were in charge of the money.

00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:48.400
You were the one who went out and said, okay, well, in order to go through this whole thing,

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.600
we need to have a building where we can work out of.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:51.800
We may need two buildings.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:57.320
So you go out and you survey the place and get everything down, and then you make that

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:58.320
part of it happen.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:10:59.320
Yes.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:01.320
And of course, the other part of the story that I love is when you get lost on the way

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:02.320
back.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:05.120
For the life of me, I couldn't find the hotel.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:06.120
Yeah.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.520
I mean, you know, amongst all the seriousness of what you're going through, all right,

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.920
where's the McDonald's that we can see?

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.320
I forgot about that, actually.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:16.320
I'll tell that.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.360
I forgot all about it.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:26.120
In January of 2002, I was named Chief of Counterterrorism Operations in Pakistan, big job.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:32.880
This was an opportunity to prove myself, to serve the country, and to exact justice

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:36.820
against the people who had killed so many Americans on 9-11.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:43.320
So I went to Islamabad, Pakistan, which is the capital, and took up my job at the

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.160
embassy there.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.760
When I first arrived, on my first day, the senior CIA officer in country told me that

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:56.880
what he wanted me to do was to come up with a standard operating procedure for taking

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:01.200
down an al-Qaeda safe house.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:04.320
I had no idea how to take down an al-Qaeda safe house.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:11.920
I went back to the office with a legal pad, and I wrote, safe house, take down.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:14.680
And I thought, okay, so if I were going to take down a safe house, what would I

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:15.680
do?

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:20.640
Well, I would want it to be dark, because I wouldn't want anybody to see me.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.980
And so I wrote at the top of the page, 0-200, figuring everybody's asleep by two

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.720
o'clock in the morning.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.720
And then I thought, well, I need battering rams.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:31.520
So we're going to order some battering rams.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:36.840
From a catalog, it's a police supply catalog, www.galls.com.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.480
You can buy anything you want.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.320
So we ordered a few cases of battering rams.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:43.920
And I thought, well, we break down the door.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:47.000
We separate the men from the women and children.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.280
And then we take the men to jail.

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:52.720
And we can interrogate them, either jail or a safe house.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:54.960
So we tried it one night.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:13:00.160
We had a tip with an address, and we broke down the door at two o'clock.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:04.160
And there was a 19-year-old Tunisian kid in there, just started crying, asked if

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:06.160
he could call his mom.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.640
And we said, no, you can't.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:13.160
And we took him to the jail in Raul Pindi and interrogated him there.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.000
And we sent him to Guantanamo.

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:18.040
And I thought, wow, that was easy enough.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.360
So we said, well, let's try it again.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:27.600
And an Egyptian intelligence officer handed me a slip of paper with an address on it.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:28.960
So I said, OK, we'll try it again.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.160
We broke down the door at 2 a.m.

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:34.280
Two guys from Egyptian Islamic jihad were in there.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:37.880
We grabbed them, interrogated them, put them on a plane to Guantanamo.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:40.600
I said, well, this job's easy.

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:44.600
But then I got a call one weekend that Abu Zubaydah was somewhere in Pakistan and that

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:45.600
we had to catch him.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:46.880
We had to take him alive.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:49.320
Abu Zubaydah was the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.080
He was the director of logistics for al-Qaeda.

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:57.720
So if you needed a fake passport, if you needed a safe house, if you needed to get

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:03.920
back to your home country or to sneak into Afghanistan, Abu Zubaydah was your guy.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:08.480
He founded both of al-Qaeda's training centers, training camps in southern Afghanistan

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:10.080
and Kandahar province.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:15.200
And he also founded the al-Qaeda safe house in Peshawar, Pakistan called the House of Martyrs.

00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:19.680
So this is a bad man, a bad guy.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.960
But you can't just say he's somewhere in Pakistan, go catch him.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:30.240
Because Pakistan is the size of Texas and it has more than 200 million people in it.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:38.240
And we knew from our NSA friends that he was constantly moving between cities, constantly.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:44.960
And so we were hot on his trail, but we were always a day or two or three behind.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:48.740
Later on when we finally captured him, we also captured his diary.

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:51.760
And he knew that we were on him.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:54.040
That's why he was moving so frequently.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:59.000
And he would write these passages, I just made it out ahead of the Americans.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:04.920
They got to the house the next day, thanks to God I will continue to fight on and

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:05.920
on and on.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:07.640
Sounds like cat and mouse.

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:09.280
That's exactly what it was.

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.120
It was cat and mouse.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:17.000
So clearly my ideas to capture him were not working.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.520
And so I asked headquarters to send me a targeting analyst.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:25.680
A targeting analyst is an unusual character at the CIA.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.280
Most analysts sit in these cubicles.

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:34.560
They think the big thoughts, they're experts on these obscure issues or on a country.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:39.120
They write papers for the president, the vice president, the secretaries of state and defense,

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.600
the national security advisor.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:45.440
A targeting analyst is completely different.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:53.160
He would take thousands, tens, hundreds of thousands of pieces of information and pour

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:59.080
through them, a lot of its metadata, to try to figure out a person's location.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.280
Now why do you want to know these locations?

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:05.760
You want to capture this guy, you want to kill the guy, you want to fire a rocket from

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.720
a drone at the guy, whatever.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:11.960
You have to locate this one person.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:15.000
Now everybody makes mistakes.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.040
Bin Laden finally made a mistake.

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:18.760
Abu Zabeda certainly made mistakes.

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:20.840
They all make mistakes.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:26.680
And in the meantime, you're recruiting human assets in the area, in that click

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:27.680
of people.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.600
An asset being someone who could give you information.

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:31.720
That's right.

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:33.200
We call those people agents.

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.840
We call ourselves officers.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:40.840
So you're recruiting agents to report back to you that Muhammad Abdullah is over here

00:16:40.840 --> 00:16:45.160
in this house and Abdul Rashid is over here at this house and then you can kick down

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:47.840
the door and take them.

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:49.560
So we chased Abu Zabeda all over the place.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.560
The analyst finally came.

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:59.640
He worked tirelessly, 18 hours a day, just writing on this giant piece of paper,

00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:07.400
writing information from the metadata that helped us to narrow the possible locations

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:08.400
for Abu Zabeda.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:14.560
He finally came to me and said, I can't narrow it down to fewer than 14 possible locations.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.920
But we had never done more than two raids before in a night, ever.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:24.240
We had never hit certainly 14 places simultaneously at two o'clock in the morning.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:30.360
So I asked headquarters to send me a plane full of people, pallets of weapons, communications

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.360
gear, walkie-talkies.

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:32.360
Another building.

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:33.360
Yeah.

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:38.200
Two million dollars in cash that I bought two buildings with.

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:39.760
Just everything we needed.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:41.440
More battering rams.

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:43.160
And they came.

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:46.120
And so we decided we were going to divide up into teams.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:52.200
I bought two safe houses, one in Faisalabad, one in a nearby city that I'm not allowed

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:54.920
to name, that was two hours east.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:55.920
Yes.

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:59.200
They let me say it's a giant city, two hours east, and there's only one city, two hours

00:17:59.200 --> 00:18:00.200
east.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:05.920
So anyway, yeah, stupid rules.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:13.400
So on March the 22nd, 2002, we hit 14 sites simultaneously.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:18.960
I'm not allowed to say the number of al-Qaeda fighters that we caught, it was many dozens.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:25.680
Then in one of those houses was Abu Zubaydah, his bodyguard, and an al-Qaeda bomb maker.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:32.920
In another house we caught both of the commanders of the two al-Qaeda training camps.

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:36.440
And Abu Zubaydah was shot trying to escape.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:39.800
He was jumping from the roof of his safe house to the roof of the neighboring house.

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:44.560
A Pakistani policeman shot him with an AK-47, hit him in the thigh, the groin in the

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:47.400
stomach, nearly killed him.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:53.360
We rushed him to a hospital, it was a gruesome scene, but they were able to save his life.

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:57.920
Let's jump ahead a little bit if we could, because we sort of know what is eventually

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.200
going to happen to some degree.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:04.160
So now I want to bring it back to when you get back here.

00:19:04.160 --> 00:19:08.280
And all the great things, all the great time that you had spent trying to figure

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:12.200
this whole thing out, and finally did figure it all out.

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:15.160
But now that's passed.

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:21.840
And now things are about to happen, that we know should not have happened to you.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:27.880
I got back to headquarters May the 6th, 2002, and I was in the cafeteria one day.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:32.440
And a senior officer from the CIA's Counterterrorism Center, which was my home office, approached

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:34.520
me very, very casually.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:37.200
And he said, oh hey, I'm glad I ran into you.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:42.240
Hey, do you want to be certified in the use of enhanced interrogation techniques?

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:45.000
I had never heard that term before.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.880
And I said, what does that mean?

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:51.640
And he said, we're going to start getting rough with these guys, very excitedly.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:53.480
I said, what's that mean?

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:57.720
And he told me these 10 techniques that had been approved by both the Justice Department

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.960
and the White House that were going to be used on all kinds.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.000
I said, man, that sounds like a torture program to me.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:06.000
And he said, no, no, justice said it's okay.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.000
The president signed off.

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:10.000
I said, I don't know.

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:13.000
I went and actually sought the counsel of a senior,

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.000
very, very senior CIA officer.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:18.000
And he agreed with me, this is a slippery slope.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.000
You don't want any part of it.

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:23.000
Of the 14 people they asked, I was the only one who said no.

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:25.000
Originally it was two of us.

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.000
And then the other one changed his mind

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:29.000
and went through the training.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:32.000
I became known as the human rights guy at the CIA

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:37.000
which was not a compliment and was not career enhancing.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:42.000
But I had a real ethical problem with a torture program.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.000
As I said, when we first started our conversation,

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:48.000
we have laws that govern this kind of thing

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.000
and torture is simply illegal.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.000
And so I declined.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:56.000
Now because we had caught Abu Zabeda,

00:20:56.000 --> 00:20:58.000
even with that declination, I was promoted

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:00.000
and I became the executive assistant

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.000
to the CIA's deputy director for operations.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:05.000
So I had access to literally every cable

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:09.000
that was being produced by every CIA officer around the world.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.000
You do nothing in your class

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:16.000
but go up, up, up as you travel into the ranks of the CIA.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:18.000
12 exceptional performance awards,

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:21.000
a sustained superior performance award,

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.000
two meritorious unit commendations.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.000
I got the medal for bravery.

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.000
I got the counter-terrorism service medal.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:30.000
So I wasn't some schmo.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:33.000
And that's the part I want you to talk about.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:35.000
That's because you're not some schmo

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:38.000
and because you do carry a lot of weight,

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:41.000
not only from a humanitarian point of view

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:45.000
but from your just general knowledge of right and wrong.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:47.000
And that's very, very important.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:50.000
Well see, this is one of the things about the CIA

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:53.000
that you've hit on that I think is really crucial.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.000
There's literally no training in ethics, right?

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.000
You have to have your own moral code

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:01.000
when you go into the CIA

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:04.000
and you have to know right and wrong

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:06.000
because they're not going to teach it to you

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.000
and you are continually confronted

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:12.000
with these moral quandaries, these dilemmas operationally

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:16.000
and you have to decide should I do A or should I do B?

00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:19.000
A is ugly but it's going to get the job done.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:22.000
B is a personal affront to me

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.000
so what do I do?

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.000
And nobody is going to tell you

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:28.000
you should do A or you should do B.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:30.000
You have to do it yourself.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.000
And that's a personal choice.

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:35.000
And there are consequences to those choices.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:37.000
And I think that another thing

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:39.000
I haven't heard you speak about at all

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:42.000
or is not mentioned in the book to any great degree

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:44.000
was your upbringing.

00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:47.000
And what it was like to be a little John Kiriago

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:50.000
and without naming places or street addresses

00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.000
or anything like that.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:54.000
But just the fact that you grew up in a very organized,

00:22:54.000 --> 00:22:56.000
very loving background.

00:22:56.000 --> 00:22:58.000
I was very fortunate.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.000
All four of my grandparents emigrated

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:02.000
from the Greek island of Rhodes.

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Primarily as economic refugees.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:11.000
Rhodes had been under Ottoman domination control

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:13.000
for almost 500 years.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:16.000
They turned it over to the Italians in 1917

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:19.000
and the Italians occupied the island until 1947.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.000
So my grandparents all left.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:26.000
And we're so grateful to this country

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:29.000
for giving them an opportunity to make something

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:31.000
not just for themselves but for their children.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:35.000
My dad and his sister were the first people

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:38.000
in their family ever to go to college.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:39.000
Ever.

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:45.000
And so the American experience

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.000
was very, very important to my grandparents.

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:50.000
I mentioned earlier in the talk at Hamilton Hall

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:53.000
that to hear my grandfather tell the story,

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:55.000
Franklin Roosevelt was waiting for him at Ellis Island

00:23:55.000 --> 00:23:58.000
to give him a job and to give him citizenship.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.000
And of course he wasn't.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.000
But until the day he died in 1978,

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.000
there was a framed picture of Franklin Roosevelt

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:07.000
on the TV.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:09.000
He was very serious about this.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:13.000
And so I came from a strongly nuclear family,

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:16.000
strongly Greek American church every Sunday,

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:18.000
Greek school Tuesdays and Thursdays.

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:21.000
You know, the Greek dances and parties and such

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.000
in the summertime.

00:24:23.000 --> 00:24:27.000
But at the same time, a love of the United States

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.000
and a desire to pay the country back

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:32.000
for what it had already given us.

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:34.000
My father served in the military, of course,

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.000
during the Korean War.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:40.000
And I only considered public service.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:41.000
Only.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:44.000
It never even occurred to me to go into the private sector

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:46.000
because all through my childhood,

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:48.000
I was told we have to pay the country back.

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:50.000
We have to pay the country back.

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:52.000
And so that's why I joined the CIA.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:54.000
I told my parents when I was nine

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.000
that I wanted to join the CIA.

00:24:56.000 --> 00:25:03.000
Yeah, that was the one, the biggest part of that book

00:25:03.000 --> 00:25:06.000
was seeing and hearing your,

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:10.000
you grow up and going to school

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:12.000
and being like the top of your class

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.000
and graduating top of your class

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:17.000
and how one of the professors took you aside

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.000
at the end of graduation and said,

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:23.000
hey, listen, I have an offer.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:25.000
There's some information that you might be interested in.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:26.000
Right.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:27.000
Which was when...

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:31.000
He was actually undercover as a professor,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:33.000
which is now illegal, by the way.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:35.000
We have the Equal Employment Opportunity Act

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:38.000
now in this country since 1993.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.000
But he was undercover as a professor

00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:44.000
looking for people who might fit into the CIA's culture.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:47.000
And he thought I would fit in.

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:51.000
And so it's up to you to pass the tests

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:54.000
and the polygraph and the background investigation

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:55.000
and all that.

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:57.000
But he certainly got the ball rolling.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.000
I'll be forever indebted to him.

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:01.000
But he was right.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:02.000
I was a good fit.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:04.000
Sort of a double-edged sword though.

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.000
It was certainly a double-edged sword.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:07.000
Yeah.

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.000
And you know, the funny thing too,

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:11.000
he told me an absolutely fascinating story one time.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:14.000
And it made me think that I was a lot more like him

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:16.000
than I had realized.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:20.000
And I think he saw it in me before I ever saw it in him.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:21.000
Right.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:24.000
He was the founder of a unit inside the CIA

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:29.000
that did psychological and psychiatric examinations

00:26:29.000 --> 00:26:32.000
by long distance, mostly of foreign leaders.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:36.000
And he was getting ready to go on vacation one time in 1971.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:38.000
And his boss came up to him and said,

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.000
I want you to do a psychological profile before you leave

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.000
and handed him a file.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:46.000
And the file was on Daniel Ellsberg.

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:50.000
Well, it's illegal for the CIA to do anything

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:53.000
with an American or against an American.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:55.000
And he said, you want me to do a psychological evaluation

00:26:55.000 --> 00:26:57.000
on an American citizen?

00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:59.000
He said, not a chance.

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:00.000
And he handed it back.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:02.000
We should state too for those who aren't aware

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:03.000
of who Daniel Ellsberg is.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:04.000
Yes.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:08.000
Daniel Ellsberg released the Pentagon Papers in 1971

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:11.000
that showed that not only were we losing the Vietnam War,

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:13.000
but our generals knew that we were losing the Vietnam War

00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:16.000
for almost a decade and had been lying to the president

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:17.000
the entire time.

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:19.000
So perhaps one of the most important whistleblowers

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:20.000
of our time.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:22.000
He's the godfather of all modern national security

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:23.000
whistleblowers.

00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:27.000
I have nothing but deep love and respect for Dan Ellsberg.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:31.000
He's been a great source of support for me.

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:36.000
So he went on vacation.

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:40.000
And when he came back, he gets a subpoena.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:43.000
And the subpoena is for this congressional committee.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:45.000
And they want to know, why did you do this psychological

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:47.000
evaluation of Daniel Ellsberg?

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:48.000
He said, I didn't.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:51.000
It turned out his boss did it and wrote his name on it.

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:53.000
So he stood up.

00:27:53.000 --> 00:27:55.000
And he ended up testifying before the Watergate Committee

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:57.000
saying, this is wrong.

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:58.000
I knew it was wrong.

00:27:58.000 --> 00:27:59.000
I refused to do it.

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:00.000
And they knew it was wrong.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.000
And they did it anyway.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:07.000
So sometimes you just have to stand up and call it like it is.

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:10.000
One of the things about the CIA is,

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.000
CIA culture is such that they want you to believe

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:16.000
that everything is a shade of gray.

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:18.000
And that's just simply not true.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.000
Some things are black and white.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:22.000
They're right and wrong.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:26.000
And for me, torture was just simply wrong.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:31.000
So all that has been exposed several years go by.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:35.000
I'll leave that to you to describe because I know you know

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:40.000
the exact dates in August and 2008 and 2009.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:44.000
So everything seemed to be fine when you're out.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:45.000
You get out.

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:49.000
And then suddenly one day everything falls apart.

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:51.000
Which time?

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:52.000
Yes.

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.000
Just prior to you being?

00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.000
Yes.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:57.000
Sent.

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:02.000
I left the CIA in 2004 and I gave an interview to ABC News in 2007

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:05.000
in which I blew the whistle on the torture program.

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:08.000
I was investigated by the FBI for a full year

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:11.000
from December of 2007 to December of 2008.

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:13.000
They determined that I had not committed a crime.

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:15.000
They closed the case.

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:18.000
I had no idea that three weeks later

00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:20.000
when Barack Obama became president

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:23.000
and John Brennan became the Deputy National Security Advisor

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:26.000
that John, an old colleague of mine,

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:31.000
asked the Justice Department to secretly reopen the case against me.

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:32.000
No idea.

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.000
They tapped my phones.

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.000
They intercepted my emails.

00:29:36.000 --> 00:29:42.000
They put groups of FBI agents on my house, on my car.

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:43.000
And then they set you up.

00:29:43.000 --> 00:29:44.000
And then they set me up.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:45.000
They tried.

00:29:45.000 --> 00:29:48.000
They tried to set me up at first.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:49.000
Yeah.

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.000
At the time I was working for John Kerry

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:54.000
on the Senate Foreign Relations Committee

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:56.000
I was the senior investigator.

00:29:56.000 --> 00:30:00.000
And the great thing about that job is you get to have

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:05.060
lunch with foreign diplomats all the time and it's a great opportunity to exchange ideas and

00:30:05.280 --> 00:30:09.140
Opinions about what's happening around the world mostly in the Middle East for me

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:15.720
So I get a call from the number three at the Japanese Embassy and he invites me to lunch. I said I'd be delighted

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:18.720
we meet at this little cafe on Capitol Hill and

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:26.440
I remember the lunch we talked about Israeli elections and Turkish elections and we talked about the Middle East peace process and

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:31.920
At the end of it he said to me so what's next for you and I said actually I think I'm gonna resign soon

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:37.640
I told senator Kerry. I'd give him two years. It's been two and a half. I have five children

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:40.680
I need to make some money so that I can put them through college and

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:45.400
He said no, don't do that. If you give me information

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:51.200
I can give you money and I said well, what's wrong with you?

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:53.240
Yeah, did he not realize who we were speaking to yeah

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:57.660
Well, I I can't believe that you would make a pitch like that. I said shame on you

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:03.720
Yeah, yeah, I got up and I walked out and I went directly to the office of the Senate security officer

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:08.240
And I said I was just pitched by a foreign intelligence officer. Oh boy

00:31:08.240 --> 00:31:13.720
He says well sit at this standalone computer write it up as a memo. I'll send it to the FBI

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:15.640
It's a long story short

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:20.880
The one who was who was trying to turn you was not someone who was doing that at all

00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:27.220
But rather a member of there was no Japanese diplomat, right? I met with him five times at the FBI's insistence

00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:30.680
It turned out that he was an FBI agent under cover

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:37.000
Trying to get me to commit espionage so that they could prosecute me and send me to prison for the rest of my life

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:41.080
on an espionage charge a real espionage charge now espionage is

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:48.700
One of the gravest crimes with which an American can be charged and sometimes carries the death penalty private sloth

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:56.180
Yes, precisely. Yeah. Yes, who was someone who was I believe World War two was it World War two World War two

00:31:56.180 --> 00:32:01.580
He was charged with espionage and he was I think the only man executed

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:05.140
He was shot shot firing squad right by firing squad, huh?

00:32:05.580 --> 00:32:10.580
